TRACE RECORD FIELDS AND EVENT INTERPRETATION

Current trace.c column headings:
  Time  PID  Command  TTY  Type  Function  Arg  Ret  Info
Time is the captured event timestamp; PID identifies the displayed
process; Command is the mapped /bin image when known; TTY may be
console, ttyS1 or ttyN0. Unknown executable IDs may show '?'.
Do not interpret a missing name as a failed operation.

TYPE / FUNCTION
  SYSCALL  name    entering the system-call wrapper
  SYSCALL  name/   returning from that syscall
  KERNEL   func    instrumented kernel-function entry
  KERNEL   func/   kernel-function return
  SWITCH   SWITCH  scheduler transition; Info is old -> new PID
  TIMER    TICKx1K  SysTick event
  OTHER    UNKNOWN  unrecognized event code

The '/' after Function marks a return, NOT a file path. Indentation
is a display-only nesting view, not an actual stack dump. The trace
renderer can combine same-timestamp function entry/exit pairs.

ARG / RET / INFO
  Arg: a numeric syscall argument, FD target, pathname prefix, or
       program name depending on the syscall. It is not always a
       complete argument list.
  Ret: '--' at entry, actual signed return at syscall exit.
  Info: up to 16 data bytes for selected read/write exits; '--'
        otherwise. A preview does not guarantee complete payload.
  File names are cached in a small table and may be truncated.
  Tracing records are finite: overwritten events may be reported.

EXAMPLE, ILLUSTRATIVE (not a captured hardware log)
  ... SYSCALL open     /tmp/x       --  --
  ... SYSCALL open/    /tmp/x        3  --
  ... SYSCALL read     3            --  --
  ... SYSCALL read/    3             0  --
The open returned FD 3; read returned EOF (0), not an error.
A negative result on read/open is an error indicator; inspect the
preceding operation, FD lifetime, path and permissions/availability.
Use /man/trace-investigation for the repeatable debugging workflow.
